-Retrieval-Augmented Generation \cite{lewis2020retrieval, guu2020realm} decoupled parametric memory from external knowledge, but standard RAG struggles with knowledge drift and multi-step reasoning. Graph-based architectures structure retrieved evidence: GraphRAG \cite{edge2024graphrag} builds hierarchical entity-relation communities for global summarization, and Think-on-Graph \cite{sun2024thinkongraph} runs iterative LLM-guided reasoning over knowledge graphs. In temporal domains, Liu and Cao \cite{liu2026raglearningunderstandinglimits} introduced a continuous-knowledge-drift benchmark and Chronos, a time-aware method organizing evidence into an Event Evolution Graph (EEG); Zhang et al. \cite{zhang2025respectingtemporalcausalconsistencyentityevent} proposed E2RAG for temporal-causal consistency across entity-event subgraphs; and Zhu et al. \cite{zhu2025rightanswerrighttime} developed STAR-RAG for time-sensitive rule graph summarization. In law, Chen et al.~\cite{chen2026legalgraphrag} proposed LegalGraphRAG, a Researcher, Auditor and Adjudicator multi-agent system over a hierarchical legal graph, and de Martim~\cite{demartim2025satgraphrag} introduced SAT-Graph RAG to trace diachronic statutory norm versions across constitutional amendments. These systems organize sources by abstraction level or model formal amendment events, whereas common-law systems also require tracking how precedent evolves across citation networks, where earlier decisions remain presumptive authority unless distinguished, limited or overruled; and static graphs retrieve structure but lack the procedural logic for multi-hop reasoning over evolving precedents.
