 \subsubsection{Data Leakage Mitigation Framework}
 \label{subsubsec:data-leakage}
-TERRA applies four safeguards against leakage. (1)~\textbf{Temporal directionality:} an earlier case cannot cite later jurisprudence, so forward \texttt{CITES} edges require $t(v_{\text{cite}}) \ge t(v_{\text{cited}})$, and retrospective invalidation uses CRS-derived \texttt{OVERRULES} relations (Section~\ref{subsec:phase1-ingestion}) rather than unrestricted backward edges, preventing the retriever from projecting modern standards onto historical inquiries. (2)~\textbf{Vector-store isolation:} benchmark queries, rubrics and reference answers are segregated from the index, which holds only opinion text and generic procedural scaffolds. (3)~\textbf{Cross-domain bleed prevention:} title matching and the proximity flag (which terminated no query in the canonical run) are coarse first-line signals, and NLI verification is designed to suppress non-entailed subgraphs before synthesis. (4)~\textbf{Temporal parametric containment:} to limit post-$T$ doctrine entering historical answers, traversals and retrieved corpora are bounded by provenance timestamps ($\text{KnowledgeProvenance} \le T$), and candidate generations $\hat{y}_{\text{candidate}}$ pass an entailment gate against contemporaneous evidence before release. In the leakage probe (Section~\ref{subsec:temporal-leakage}) the gate made six interventions, but its bulk leakage reduction was not statistically established.
